B2 – Adversarial Inputs and Safety

 B2.1 – Long Donor Name

Using `scanf("%s", name)` may cause buffer overflow if the donor name is too long. Using `fgets()` is safer because it limits the input size.

### B2.2 – Missing File

If the file cannot be opened, `fopen()` returns `NULL`. We should check this and use `perror()` to display the error message.

### B2.3 – Invalid Command-Line Arguments

Check the number of arguments using `argc` before accessing `argv`. If the arguments are incorrect, display a usage message.

### B2.4 – Memory Safety

Use Valgrind or AddressSanitizer to detect memory leaks, invalid memory access, and memory errors. Free dynamically allocated memory after use.

### B2.5 – Alternative

Using `getline()` allows reading long input dynamically. It reduces buffer overflow risks and is suitable for variable-length donor names.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    char name[50];

    if (argc != 2)
    {
        printf("Usage: ./B2 <filename>\n");
        return 1;
    }

    FILE *fp = fopen(argv[1], "r");

    if (fp == NULL)
    {
        perror("File error");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("File opened successfully.\n");
    fclose(fp);

    printf("Enter donor name: ");
    fgets(name, sizeof(name), stdin);

    printf("Name: %s", name);
    return 0;
}
